\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The alternating cycle at zero mass}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{The [alternating-cycle eigenbasis](fdip-0037) has constant unit
vector #{u_0=(1,1,1,1)/2}; its positive eigenvalues are
#{2,2t,2+2t}. Hence the [mean-zero law](fdip-000C) has
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{pdet}L&=8t(1+t),\\
Z_H&=\frac{(2\pi)^{3/2}}{\sqrt{8t(1+t)}},\\
L^+&=\tfrac12P_{u_1}+\tfrac1{2t}P_{u_2}
            +\tfrac1{2+2t}P_{u_3}.
\end{aligned}}
Its covariance rows follow from the four signed sums of \ref{fdip-0037}
by setting #{d_0=0}, #{d_1=1/2}, #{d_2=1/(2t)}, and
#{d_3=1/(2+2t)}. This is the [pseudoinverse](fdip-0032), not an inverse
on the full vertex space.}
\p{At #{t=1}, its first row is
##{(5/16,-1/16,-3/16,-1/16).}
All diagonal entries are #{5/16}. Thus an opposite field difference has
variance #{2(5/16)-2(-3/16)=1}, while an adjacent difference has variance
#{2(5/16)-2(-1/16)=3/4}. Both are finite limits of the corresponding
massive variances by \ref{fdip-0034}.}
\p{The massive constant contribution to every covariance entry is
#{1/(4m^2)}. Its divergence cancels in field differences, and
[kernel factorization](fdip-0033) gives the exact normalizer relation
##{\begin{aligned}
Z_{Q_m}&=\frac{\sqrt{2\pi}}{m}Z_{H,m},\\
mZ_{Q_m}&\longrightarrow\sqrt{2\pi}Z_H.
\end{aligned}}
The full normalizer therefore does not tend to #{Z_H}; the constrained
law uses its own three-dimensional reference volume.}
\p{This calculation applies the induced-volume construction of
\ref{fdip-000C} to the positive spectral modes. The positive Gaussian
density ingredient is
\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505}{friedli2017statistical}; the zero-mode
removal and its normalization factor are derived above.}
